@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In BASIC, variable names use @code{case_insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{UPPER_CASE}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: IF, THEN, ELSE, ENDIF, WHILE, WEND, FOR, TO, STEP, NEXT, DO, LOOP, UNTIL, EXIT, SUB, END SUB, FUNCTION, END FUNCTION, CALL, RETURN, GOSUB, RETURN
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, ^, =, <>, <, >, <=, >=, AND, OR, NOT
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) or single quotes (@code{'...'})
@item Line numbers: Required for GOTO/GOSUB targets (1-99999)
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A BASIC program consists of numbered lines that are executed sequentially unless altered by control flow statements.
Programs may include SUB and FUNCTION procedures for modular code organization.

@example
10 REM --- Initialize game ---
20 SCORE = 0
30 LIVES = 3
40 CURRENT_ROOM = 1
50 WHILE LIVES > 0
60   GOSUB 1000          @r{Process player input}
70   GOSUB 2000          @r{Update game state}
80   GOSUB 3000          @r{Render display}
90 WEND
100 END
1000 @r{Input processing subroutine}
1010 INPUT "Command> "; CMD$
1020 RETURN
2000 @r{Game state update}
2010 IF CMD$ = "MOVE NORTH" THEN CURRENT_ROOM = CURRENT_ROOM + 10
2020 RETURN
3000 @r{Rendering subroutine}
3010 CLS
3020 PRINT "You are in room "; CURRENT_ROOM
3030 RETURN
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

BASIC supports object-oriented programming through user-defined types and procedure pointers,
though traditional EIGHTBOL BASIC usage focuses on procedural code with global data.

@example
TYPE ObjectType
    x AS INTEGER
    y AS INTEGER
    sprite AS INTEGER
END TYPE

DECLARE SHARED player AS ObjectType

SUB MovePlayer (dx AS INTEGER, dy AS INTEGER)
    player.x = player.x + dx
    player.y = player.y + dy
END SUB
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a SUB procedure or function that takes no arguments:

@example
CALL InitializeGraphics
CALL ClearScreen
CALL PlayStartupMusic
END SUB
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value:

@example
score = CalculateBonus(level, time_left)
ifactor = GetInputFactor()
volume = GetMasterVolume()
END SUB
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in BASIC's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a SUB procedure with parameters:

@example
CALL DrawSprite(sprite_id, x_pos, y_pos)
CALL PlaySound(sound_id, volume, loop_flag)
CALL CheckCollision(obj1, obj2, result_var)
END SUB
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

BASIC accesses library routines through standard CALL statements or function invocations:

@example
CALL LoadLevel(level_number)          @r{Direct call}
CALL FarProc(library_address)         @r{Far call}
result = LibraryFunction(input)       @r{Library function}
OBJECT.Method param1, param2          @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: CALL ProcedureName(...)
@item Far: CALL AbsoluteAddress(...)
@item Library: CALL LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: OBJECT.MethodName(...)
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in BASIC are called using absolute addresses or through
DECLARE statements that specify the memory bank:

@example
DECLARE SUB FarLoadLIBRARY SEGMENT
CALL FarLoadLIBRARY(0x8000)         @r{Call routine in bank 1}
END SUB
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: CALL AbsoluteAddress or DECLARE SUB ... SEGMENT
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in BASIC uses dot notation on user-defined types:

@example
TYPE SpriteObject
    x AS INTEGER
    y AS INTEGER
    DECLARE SUB Draw ()
END TYPE

DECLARE SHARED player AS SpriteObject

player.Draw                     @r{Invoke object method}
player.x = 100                  @r{Set object property}
END SUB
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties
@item Far: Not applicable for methods
@item Library: Draw method may call library routines
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the LET keyword (optional) or direct assignment:

@example
LET score = 0                   @r{Explicit assignment}
lives = 3                       @r{Implicit assignment}
counter% = counter% + 1         @r{Integer variable}
message$ = "Hello, World!"      @r{String variable}
END SUB
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties of user-defined types) are assigned using dot notation:

@example
player.x = 160                  @r{Set player's x coordinate}
player.y = 100                  @r{Set player's y coordinate}
player.sprite = 5               @r{Set player's sprite number}
player.visible = -1             @r{Set visibility flag (TRUE)}
END SUB
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Array elements are assigned using parentheses for indexing:

@example
DIM scores(10) AS INTEGER       @r{Declare array of 11 integers}
DIM names$(5) AS STRING         @r{Declare array of 6 strings}
DIM board(8,8) AS INTEGER       @r{Declare 2D array}

scores(0) = 100                 @r{Set first element}
names$(1) = "Alice"             @r{Set second element}
board(3,4) = 1                  @r{Set matrix element}
END SUB
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses standard infix operators:

@example
score = score + 10              @r{Addition}
lives = lives - 1               @r{Subtraction}
total = (width * height) + depth @r{Combined operations}
delta = new_value - old_value   @r{Subtraction}
END SUB
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting operations for multiplication/division by powers of two:

@example
result = value << 2             @r{Multiply by 4 (value * 2^2)}
result = value >> 1             @r{Divide by 2 (value \ 2^1)}
result = value << 3             @r{Multiply by 8}
result = value >> 4             @r{Divide by 16}
END SUB
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
total = (base_salary * 12) + bonus - deductions
average = (sum1 + sum2 + sum3) / 3
distance = SQR((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2)
END SUB
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use standard comparison operators:

@example
IF score > 1000 THEN ...        @r{Greater than}
IF score < 50 THEN ...          @r{Less than}
IF score = 100 THEN ...         @r{Equality}
IF score <> 0 THEN ...          @r{Inequality}
IF (x >= 0) AND (x < MAX_X) THEN ... @r{Combined conditions}
IF (button = BUTTON_A) OR (button = BUTTON_B) THEN ... @r{Alternative conditions}
END SUB
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF blocks:

@example
IF temperature > THOT THEN
    status$ = "HOT"
ELSEIF temperature > TCOLD THEN
    status$ = "WARM"
ELSE
    status$ = "COLD"
ENDIF

SELECT CASE answer
    CASE "Y", "y"
        PRINT "Yes"
    CASE "N", "n"
        PRINT "No"
    CASE ELSE
        PRINT "Invalid response"
END SELECT
END SUB
@end example

BASIC does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; SELECT CASE serves
a similar purpose for multi-way branching.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using FOR/NEXT, WHILE/WEND, and DO/LOOP constructs:

@example
' FOR/NEXT loop (known iterations)
FOR i = 0 TO 9 STEP 1
    PRINT "Count:"; i
NEXT i

' WHILE/WEND loop (pre-test)
WHILE NOT EOF(1)
    LINE INPUT #1, line$
    PRINT line$
WEND

' DO/LOOP UNTIL (post-test)
DO
    INPUT "Continue? (Y/N) "; response$
LOOP UNTIL UCASE$(response$) = "Y"

' DO/LOOP WHILE (pre-test variant)
DO WHILE NOT game_over
    CALL ProcessFrame
LOOP
END SUB
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using EXIT FOR, EXIT DO, or EXIT WHILE:

@example
' Exit FOR loop early
FOR i = 0 TO 100
    IF array(i) = TARGET_VALUE THEN
        PRINT "Found at index:"; i
        EXIT FOR                    @r{Exit loop immediately}
    END IF
NEXT i

' Exit DO loop early
DO
    INPUT "Command> "; cmd$
    IF cmd$ = "QUIT" THEN
        EXIT DO                     @r{Exit loop immediately}
    END IF
    CALL ProcessCommand(cmd$)
LOOP

' Nested loop control
OUTER: FOR i = 1 TO 10
    INNER: FOR j = 1 TO 10
        IF matrix(i,j) = 0 THEN
            EXIT INNER              @r{Exit inner loop only}
        END IF
    NEXT j
NEXT i
END SUB
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

BASIC does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own internal procedures. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, BASIC commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
@r{Original BASIC code}
IF (x > 100) AND (x < 200) THEN
    CALL ShowMessage(MSG_WELCOME)
END IF

@r{Desugared via semantic-sugar layer}
IF ((x > 100) AND (x < 200)) THEN
    (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
END IF
END SUB
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
' Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
DEF SEG = &HB800                @r{Set segment to video memory}
POKE (offset * 2), tile_num     @r{Set tile number}
POKE ((offset * 2) + 1), attr   @r{Set tile attribute}

' Library routine approach
CALL LoadTileSet(tileset_id)
CALL PlaceTile(layer, x, y, tile_index)
CALL ScrollBackground(dx, dy)
END SUB
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
' Hardware sprite approach (if available)
CALL InitSprites
CALL SetSpritePosition(sprite_id, x, y)
CALL SetSpriteImage(sprite_id, image_id)
CALL ShowSprite(sprite_id, -1)   @r{-1 = visible}

' Software sprite approach
DIM sprite_x(10) AS INTEGER
DIM sprite_y(10) AS INTEGER
DIM sprite_frame(10) AS INTEGER

sprite_x(i) = 100
sprite_y(i) = 50
sprite_frame(i) = (sprite_frame(i) + 1) MOD 4
END SUB
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
' Using user-defined types
TYPE GameObject
    x AS INTEGER
    y AS INTEGER
    width AS INTEGER
    height AS INTEGER
    active AS INTEGER
END TYPE

DIM objects(20) AS GameObject

' Initialize object
objects(i).x = 100
objects(i).y = 100
objects(i).width = 16
objects(i).height = 16
objects(i).active = -1   @r{TRUE}

' Update object position
objects(i).x = objects(i).x + dx
objects(i).y = objects(i).y + dy

' Check active status
IF objects(i).active THEN
    CALL DrawObject(objects(i))
END IF
END SUB
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following BASIC features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (OPEN, CLOSE, INPUT#, PRINT#)
@item Graphics commands (LINE, CIRCLE, PAINT, GET, PUT)
@item Hardware-specific features (JOYSTICK, PEN, LIGHTPEN)
@item Extended memory handling (EMM, XMS)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution
@item Exception handling (ON ERROR RESUME NEXT)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#DEFINE, #IFDEF)
@end table

